.TH ORIGINFS 4
.SH NAME
originfs \- webfs as one origin's web page may use it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "web/originfs.m";
originfs := load Originfs Originfs->PATH;

serve:  fn(fd: ref Sys->FD, origin: string, webfs: string,
           ready: chan of string);
origin: fn(url: string): string;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
A web page's JavaScript realm (see
.IR js (1)
and
.IR charon (1))
does not see
.IR webfs (4).
While it confines itself it starts
.I originfs
in front of webfs and mounts it at
.BR /mnt/web .
Nothing else of the network is in the realm's name space, so what
.I originfs
does not let through, neither a script nor a bug in the engine can reach.
.PP
.I Serve
answers 9P on
.IR fd ,
for a page of the given
.IR origin ,
using the webfs mounted at
.IR webfs .
It forks its own name space and keeps none of its caller's file
descriptors, then sends
.B nil
on
.I ready
(or why it could not start) and serves until
.I fd
hangs up.
.I Origin
gives a URL's origin as the Fetch standard serializes it:
.IB scheme :// host\f1,
with the port only when it is not the scheme's;
.B null
for a URL that is not
.B http
or
.BR https .
.SH FILES
.TF N/contenttype
.TP
.B clone
Reading it makes a request directory and gives its number.
.TP
.B cookies
The cookies of webfs's jar this origin's documents may see, in webfs's
form: those whose domain matches the page's host, never HttpOnly ones,
Secure ones only for an
.B https
page.  Writing a line in the same form sets a cookie for the page's
host, or for a domain above it that is not a top-level one; an HttpOnly
cookie, another site's, or a Secure one from an
.B http
page is refused.
.TP
.IB N /ctl
Written before the request is made:
.BI url " url"
(http or https only),
.BI method " method"
(GET, HEAD, POST, PUT, DELETE, PATCH or OPTIONS),
.BI header " Name: value"
(the Fetch standard's forbidden request headers, such as Cookie,
Origin, Host and Referer, are dropped),
.BI mode " mode"
.RB ( cors ,
the default,
.B no-cors
or
.BR same-origin ),
and
.BI credentials " c"
.RB ( omit ,
.BR same-origin ,
the default, or
.BR include ).
.TP
.IB N /postbody
The request body.
.TP
.IB N /body\fR,\fP " status" "\fR,\fP url" "\fR,\fP contenttype" "\fR,\fP header"
The response, as
.IR webfs (4)
gives it, once
.I originfs
has let it through; reading one makes the request.  A response withheld
has an empty body and header and a
.B status
of
.BI error: " why"\f1.
.SH RULES
A request to the page's own origin goes as it is.  Cookies go when the
credentials are
.BR include ,
or
.B same-origin
and the request is to the page's origin.
.PP
A cross-origin
.B cors
request carries the page's Origin, is preceded by a preflight when its
method or headers are not CORS-safelisted, and is given back only if
the response's Access-Control-Allow-Origin names the page's origin (or
is
.B *
for a request without credentials, and Access-Control-Allow-Credentials
is
.B true
for one with them).  Only the safelisted response headers and those
Access-Control-Expose-Headers names are given.
.PP
A cross-origin
.B no-cors
request (a classic script the page loads) must use GET, HEAD or POST,
and is given back unless it is HTML, XML or JSON, by its content type or
by how its body begins, as Opaque Response Blocking has it: a page may
run another site's script, not read another site's documents.  Only its
Content-Type header is given.
.PP
A
.B same-origin
request to another origin fails.  A redirect is judged by where it ends.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/web/originfs.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR js (1),
.IR charon (1),
.IR webfs (4)
.SH BUGS
SameSite is not applied: webfs's jar does not keep it.  Cookies are not
partitioned by the page that embeds a request.  Content-Security-Policy
is not applied.
